				\begin{document}$$(\alpha {\text{h}}\nu )^{{\text{n}}} = {\text{A}}({\text{h}}\nu - {\text{Eg}})$$\end{document}(αhν)n=A(hν-Eg)where α is the absorption coefficient, hν is the photon energy (where h is Planck’s constant and ν is the frequency), Eg is band gap, and A is a proportionality constant, n depends on the nature of the electronic transition (direct or indirect):For direct transitions, n = 0.546. The point where the line drawn through the linear region of the plot intersects with the x-axis gives Eg. As shown in Fig. 7c, the band gap is 2.94 eV, confirming PL result as well as indicating that wavelengths below 400 nm are sufficient to excite valence band electrons; thus, Ce-MOF is active in the UV region.

The sonophotocatalytic degradation mechanism of the Ce-MOF is proposed in Fig. S4, when the Ce-MOF is simultaneously exposed to ultrasound and light, electrons transition from the valence band to the conduction band, leaving holes in the valence band. The conduction band position of the Ce-MOF is − 0.35 eV, which is more negative than the O2/O2− (− 0.33 eV) reduction potential, allowing photogenerated electrons to react with oxygen to form superoxide radicals (⋅O2− ). The valence band (VB) of the Ce-MOF is + 2.59 eV, which is less positive than the OH⁻/⋅OH (1.99 eV) oxidation potential, leading to the generation of ⋅OH through reactions of holes (h+) with H₂O. The π-π conjugate structure present in the organic ligand of Ce-MOF facilitates the rapid transfer of photogenerated electrons from holes to the Ce center site of the Ce-MOF, where they react with adsorbed TC. Photogenerated electrons and holes on the Ce-MOF surface oxidize O₂ and H₂O into ⋅O2− and ⋅OH46. These free radicals ultimately oxidize TC molecules to produce CO₂ and H₂O. The overall reaction mechanism is outlined in the following formulas (Eq. 5–9):5\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
